.TH MU2EDAQ-POWER-RECOVERY-BOOTSTRAP 1 "2026-09-29" "mu2edaq-power-recovery 0.2.0" "Mu2e DAQ Operations"
.SH NAME
bootstrap.sh, bootstrap.ps1, start-mu2edaq-power-recovery.sh,
start-mu2edaq-power-recovery.ps1, stop-mu2edaq-power-recovery.sh,
stop-mu2edaq-power-recovery.ps1 \- set up and control a recovery run
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ./bootstrap.sh
.RB [ \-\-with\-cpp ]
.br
.B ./bootstrap.ps1
.RB [ \-WithCpp ]
.br
.B ./start-mu2edaq-power-recovery.sh
.RI [ options ...]
.br
.B ./start-mu2edaq-power-recovery.ps1
.RI [ options ...]
.br
.B ./stop-mu2edaq-power-recovery.sh
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.RB [ \-\-status ]
.br
.B ./stop-mu2edaq-power-recovery.ps1
.RB [ \-Force ]
.RB [ \-Status ]
.RB [ \-Grace
.IR SECONDS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.SS bootstrap.sh, bootstrap.ps1
Creates or refreshes the project's Python virtual environment in
.IR venv/ ,
installs the package in editable mode with its dependencies, regenerates
.I requirements.txt
from what was actually resolved, and creates the
.IR logs/ ", " data/ ", " html/ " and " core/
directories.
.PP
It is idempotent and quick when there is nothing to do, which matters because
phase 0 of
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1)
calls it whenever a self-update touched
.IR pyproject.toml ,
.I requirements.txt
or the C++ sources.
.PP
.B \-\-with\-cpp
additionally configures and builds
.BR libmu2eprobe (3)
with CMake. That library is optional; without it the tools use a pure-Python
reachability fallback.
.PP
.B MU2EDAQ_PYTHON
selects the interpreter (default
.BR python3 );
Python 3.9 is the minimum.
.B VENV_DIR
relocates the environment.
.PP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
prints the script's header comment as usage and exits. The PowerShell form
takes
.B \-WithCpp
instead of
.BR \-\-with\-cpp ,
and documents itself through
.BR Get\-Help .
.SS start-mu2edaq-power-recovery.sh, start-mu2edaq-power-recovery.ps1
Bootstraps the environment if it is missing, then passes every argument
straight to
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1).
This is the entry point to use from a fresh clone, because it works before the
venv exists.
.PP
With no arguments it runs
.B \-\-phase assess
\(em the read-only survey \(em on the principle that an accidental bare
invocation should look at the cluster rather than change it.
.PP
It writes no pid file and installs no trap: the shell version simply
.BR exec s
the driver. The driver itself refuses a second concurrent run, which would
drive the same cluster from two directions and interleave power commands, by
taking an exclusive operating-system lock on
.I run.lock_file
.RI ( logs/power-recovery.lock ;
see
.BR mu2edaq-power-recovery.yaml (5))
for every invocation that can act on hardware. The second run exits 2 naming
the holder. The kernel releases the lock however a run ends, so nothing stale
is left to clean up. (The old pid file was left behind by every normal run,
because a shell trap does not survive
.BR exec ,
and after pid reuse it named an unrelated process. The old PowerShell wrapper
recorded its own pid, so stopping it orphaned the driver.)
.B VENV_DIR
relocates the environment in both versions.
.PP
Neither script has options of its own: every argument is handed to
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1),
so
.B \-\-help
and
.B \-\-version
reach the driver rather than the wrapper. The two behave identically; the
PowerShell version differs only in calling
.I venv\eScripts\emu2e-power-recovery.exe
rather than
.IR venv/bin/mu2e-power-recovery .
.SS stop-mu2edaq-power-recovery.sh
Stops a running recovery. SIGTERM first, then SIGKILL after a grace period
.RB ( GRACE ,
default 30 seconds), and only if it is needed.
.PP
The target comes from the run lock, never from a bare pid file:
.B python \-m mu2edaq_power_recovery.runlock pid
prints a pid only while the lock is held, and a free lock means any recorded
pid is stale, so nothing is signalled. Before every signal the process's
command line
.RB ( "ps \-o args= \-p" )
must still contain
.I mu2edaq_power_recovery
or
.IR mu2e-power ;
otherwise the script refuses with exit status 1. A recycled pid is therefore
never signalled. The lock file is left in place.
.B VENV_DIR
selects the Python used to run the helper,
.B LOCK_FILE
overrides
.IR run.lock_file .
.PP
.B SIGTERM
is a clean stop. The driver's handler raises the same interrupt as Ctrl-C:
queued nodes and mesh sources are cancelled rather than waited for, the
interruption is recorded, the run is marked
.IR interrupted ,
and the private Kerberos caches are destroyed at once. Commands already running
on nodes finish in the background before the process exits, which can take up
to
.IR ssh.command_timeout ;
if the grace period expires first, SIGKILL follows, after cleanup has run.
.PP
.B SIGKILL
is not clean: the interruption is not recorded, the run is left as
.IR running ,
and if it arrived before the interrupt was handled the caches the run minted,
including any root-capable service tickets, survive it. Check with
.B klist \-l
and destroy what is left by name with
.BR "kdestroy \-c" .
.TP
.B \-\-force
Skip the grace period and kill at once (SIGKILL). The run store does not record
the interruption and the cleanup does not run.
.TP
.B \-\-status
Report whether a run is active (the lock holder's pid, start time, host and
command line, or "not running" with any stale record) and change nothing.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the script's header comment as usage and exit.
.PP
Any other argument is rejected with exit status 2, rather than being passed on
to something that might act on it.
.SS stop-mu2edaq-power-recovery.ps1
The Windows equivalent, with the same two-stage behaviour: it asks the driver
to close, waits out the grace period, and only then terminates the process.
Its parameters follow PowerShell convention rather than the shell script's.
It takes the driver's pid from the same helper and verifies the process's
command line with
.B Get\-CimInstance Win32_Process
before each stop.
.TP
.B \-Force
Terminate immediately, without the grace period. The run store may not record
the interruption.
.TP
.B \-Status
Report whether a run is active and change nothing.
.TP
.BI \-Grace " SECONDS"
How long to wait before terminating. Default: 30. The shell script takes the
same value from the
.B GRACE
environment variable instead, which is the one difference between the two.
.TP
.BI \-LockFile " PATH"
Lock file to examine; default
.IR run.lock_file .
The shell script takes
.B LOCK_FILE
from the environment.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I venv/
The virtual environment.
.TP
.I logs/power-recovery.lock
The run lock
.RI ( run.lock_file ).
Holds the JSON record
.RI { pid ", " started_at ", " cmdline ", " host }
of the last run that took it; believed only while the lock is held.
.TP
.I requirements.txt
Regenerated by
.BR bootstrap.sh .
Edit
.I pyproject.toml
instead.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.EX
\&./bootstrap.sh
\&./bootstrap.sh \-\-with\-cpp
MU2EDAQ_PYTHON=python3.9 ./bootstrap.sh

\&./start-mu2edaq-power-recovery.sh                       # survey only
\&./start-mu2edaq-power-recovery.sh \-\-phase all \-\-simulate
\&./stop-mu2edaq-power-recovery.sh \-\-status
GRACE=90 ./stop-mu2edaq-power-recovery.sh
.EE
.RE
.PP
The same three steps on Windows:
.RS
.EX
\&.\ebootstrap.ps1 \-WithCpp
\&.\estart\-mu2edaq\-power\-recovery.ps1 \-\-phase all \-\-simulate
\&.\estop\-mu2edaq\-power\-recovery.ps1 \-Status
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1),
.BR mu2edaq-power-recovery (7)
